.\"
.TH GETENV 3 "March 20, 1987"
.AT 3
.SH NAME
getenv, putenv, setenv, unsetenv \- manipulate environmental variables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B char *getenv(name)
.B char *name;
.PP
.B int setenv(name, value, overwrite)
.B char *name, value;
.B int overwrite;
.PP
.B int putenv(string)
.B char *string;
.PP
.B int unsetenv(name)
.B char *name;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fIGetenv\fP searches the environment list (see \fIenviron\fP(7))
for a string of the form \fIname\fP\fB=\fP\fIvalue\fP and returns
a pointer to the string \fIvalue\fP if such a string is present,
and 0 (NULL) if it is not.
.PP
\fISetenv\fP searches the environment list as \fIgetenv\fP does; if
the string \fIname\fP is not found, a string of the form
\fIname\fP\fB=\fP\fIvalue\fP is added to the environment.  If it is
found, and \fIoverwrite\fP is non-zero, its value is changed to
\fIvalue\fP.  \fISetenv\fP returns 0 on success and -1 on failure,
where failure is caused by an inability to allocate space for the
environment.
.PP
\fIPutenv\fP installs the caller's \fIstring\fP directly in the environment.
The string must have the form \fIname\fP\fB=\fP\fIvalue\fP.  Later changes to
the caller's string change the environment, so the caller must preserve the
storage while the entry remains installed.  \fIPutenv\fP returns 0 on success
and -1 on failure.
.PP
\fIUnsetenv\fP removes all occurrences of the string \fIname\fP from
the environment and returns 0 on success or -1 on failure.  There is no
library provision for completely removing
the current environment.  It is suggested that the following code be
used to do so.
.PP
.nf
	static char	*envinit[1];
	extern char	**environ;
	environ = envinit;
.fi
.PP
For historical compatibility, \fIgetenv\fP treats the first equals sign in
\fIname\fP as the end of the name.  \fISetenv\fP and \fIunsetenv\fP require a
nonempty name without an equals sign.  \fISetenv\fP permits a leading equals
sign in \fIvalue\fP.
.SH ERRORS
\fISetenv\fP and \fIunsetenv\fP set \fIerrno\fP to EINVAL when \fIname\fP is
empty or contains an equals sign.  \fIPutenv\fP sets EINVAL when \fIstring\fP
does not contain a nonempty name followed by an equals sign.  A routine that
cannot allocate the environment vector or an owned entry sets ENOMEM.
.SH SEE ALSO
csh(1), sh(1), execve(2), environ(7)
